Article 23
(1) The Regional Government develops cooperation patterns for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is conducted between the Regional Government and:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regional Governments;
d. Higher Education Institutions;
e. Social and Religious Institutions;
f. Non-Governmental Organizations; and/or
g. Media.
(4) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.
(5) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 14
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, include:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs, and carrying out cooperation activities in the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating a budget for the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of acts of violence.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes protection action programs and activities for women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 4
The scope of regulation for the protection of women and children from acts of violence includes:
a. implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. obligations and responsibilities;
c. forms of violence;
d. rights of women and children from acts of violence;
e. institutional matters;
f. cooperation and partnership;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. awards; and
i. funding;

CHAPTER IV
FORMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 17
Forms of violence against women and children include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. maltreatment;
f. exploitation; and/or
g. other violence.

Article 2
The protection of women and children from acts of violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. public order and legal certainty;
e. transparency; and
f. protection.

CHAPTER VIII
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 25
(1) The Regional Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. coordination;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.

